# Shell scripts are executed inside Linux containers. On a Windows clone with the
# default `core.autocrlf=true`, `docker-entrypoint.sh` checks out with CRLF, the
# shebang becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the kernel looks for an interpreter called
# `/bin/sh\r` — so the backend container boot-loops with
# "exec /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory".
# Nothing in the build can recover from this, so pin the ending here.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The mirror image of the rule above: cmd.exe parses a batch file line by line and a
# lone LF can leave a trailing character on the command, so the Windows launcher is
# pinned the other way regardless of the cloner's autocrlf setting.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
